six month old Matthew sits on his  mother's lap a researcher sits opposite  and keeps his attention on a colorful  toy while a tape plays two sounds one is  baa and the other is da a sound only  used in Hindi and one which most  english-speaking listeners can't  distinguish from the English sound da  during the initial training period a toy  bunny is lit up in a large glass box to  the side of Matthew the instant the da  sound is played when the bass sound is  played the bunny stops and Matthew turns  back to the researcher the routine  continues until Matthew learns to  associate the change from ba to da with  the bunny  Matthew even turns his head in  anticipation when the researchers delay  the bunnies appearance matthew is now  ready for the test phase of the  experiment a new tape is played this  time with two different da sounds that  are only used in Hindi even though they  are two completely different sounds an  adult who can't speak Hindi doesn't hear  the difference but Matthew immediately  turns his head towards the button when  he hears the change from one Hindi da to  the other although he has never heard  Hindi spoken researchers recognized that  at six months he is still the universal  listener able to distinguish all sounds  Emma who is four months older than  Matthew has come to the lab for the  first time after a similar training  routine is Matthew she's presented with  the same problem of distinguishing the  two different Hindi da sounds  but at this age Emma whose parents speak  only English doesn't react when there is  a change from one to the other she is  more interested in the toy in front of  her than the bunny this leads the  researchers to conclude that by 10  months the baby humans stops being the  universal listener and like the adult of  the species no longer hears the  difference between the two sounds
 

